Now update ExperienceService for selected model + async runs. This is central.

Add dependency `connection: ConnectionService`. Options include. For tests, we can pass a fake connection object implementing `resolveModel`/`chooseDefaultModel`/`discovery`. Define interface `EngineSelector` in service or separate.

```ts
export interface EngineSelector {
  resolveModel(selection: ModelRef|null, discovery?: OpenCodeDiscovery): Promise<ModelResolution|null>;
  chooseDefaultModel(legacyModel?: string|null): Promise<ModelResolution|null>;
  discovery(options?): Promise<OpenCodeDiscovery>;
  status(): ConnectionStatus;
}
```

ExperienceService:
- constructor(..., options: {..., connection: EngineSelector, runManager?})
- `ensureModel(project)`: 
  - try `connection.resolveModel(project.engine.model, discovery?)`. If found return.
  - if project.engine.model is null: `chooseDefaultModel(project.engine.legacyModel)`; if found, update engine in same execution? For Maker, we can use selection and persist `engine` in the same workflow.execute that queues operation. But if model null and chooseDefault returns, set engine model with source auto/legacy. For clarity, `selectionFor(project)` returns `{model, source, shouldPersist}`.
  - if project.engine.model set but unavailable -> throw ExperienceFailure("MODEL_UNAVAILABLE", 409, false).
  - if no model -> throw MODEL_SELECTION_REQUIRED.
  - validate variant. `connection.validateSelection`.
- All `speak` calls need model+agent. `instructions` already. Determine agent from discovery roles:
  - `const discovery = await connection.discovery({selected:model})`; find role mapping; use `agent` if available else default.
  - Could cache via connection.
- `speak` calls: pass model, agent.
- `perform` calls: pass model, agent, onSession callback.
- `runMaker`: 
  ```ts
  const payload = {intent:"maker"};
  claim...
  const resolution = await this.modelFor(project);
  const discovery = await this.connection.discovery({selected:resolution.model});
  const agent = roleAgent(discovery, "maker");
  const queued = await workflow.execute(actionId, payload, expectedRevision, current => {
     const next = structuredClone(current);
     next.engine = {model: resolution.model..., source: resolution.source==="selected"? current.engine.source : resolution.source, updatedAt: now, legacyModel: current.engine.legacyModel}
     next.operations.push({..., status:"QUEUED", model, agent, startedAt: now, finishedAt:null, result:"Queued for OpenCode."});
     rememberTranscript(next,"user",`Queued Maker run with ${model.key}${variant}`);
     return refreshNextAction(next);
  });
  const operationId = queued.project.operations.at(-1)!.id;
  this.runManager.start({operationId, projectId, payload, actionId, sessionId, prompt, workspace, model, agent});
  return {...queued, accepted:true};
  ```
  But claim before model resolution: `claim` checks action id/revision but doesn't reserve. If two requests same action, both could start. Since HTTP single process, but still. We need atomic enqueue. Use `workflow.execute` directly; it checks action id. We can skip `claim` or claim then execute. `claim` returns duplicate if action exists. Good. Then execute; if conflict due to concurrent same action, one gets duplicate. Race unlikely. Better `workflow.execute` handles idempotency.
  - `runManager.start` in-process; if it throws? It shouldn't.
  - Return 202.
- `RunManager` needs service methods. We can put manager logic inside ExperienceService for simplicity: maintain `activeRuns` Map and methods `startMaker`, `completeMaker`, `cancel`. But cleaner separate file. Let's create `src/server/experience/runs.ts` with `RunCoordinator` that takes `{guide, workflow, safeDirectory, demoWorkspace}` and handles background. But it needs `instructions` prompt. ExperienceService can pass prompt.

Define:
```ts
export interface MakerRunInput {
 operationId: string; projectId: string; actionId: string; payload: object;
 sessionId: string|null; prompt: string; workspace: string; model: ModelRef; agent: string|null;
}
export class MakerRunCoordinator {
 private active = new Map<string, {sessionId: string|null; promise: Promise<void>}>();
 start(input) { ... }
 cancel(operationId) ...
 isActive(id)
 async complete(...)
}
```
- start:
```ts
const state = {sessionId: input.sessionId, promise: Promise.resolve()};
this.active.set(id,state);
state.promise = this.execute(input,state).finally(()=>this.active.delete(id));
```
- execute:
  - update operation RUNNING: `workflow.executeLatest(`run:${id}:start`, {phase:"start"}, project => patch)`. If duplicate action (server restarted?), update mutation may not run; but operation queued. Use action id unique per phase.
  - `onSession` callback updates session and operation nativeSessionId (RUNNING) via executeLatest `run:${id}:session`.
  - `guide.perform`.
  - edited check.
  - node check.
  - complete via `executeLatest(run:${id}:complete, payload)`:
    - find op; if CANCELLED don't overwrite? If user cancelled while running, status CANCELLED; completion should not mark SUCCEEDED. Check `if (op.status === "CANCELLED") return next;` and add transcript "completed after cancellation"? Better mark `CANCELLED` and record diff in result maybe. If cancelled, still record diff? Could append result. Keep CANCELLED and result includes "Cancellation was recorded before completion; native diff observed: ...". 
    - set model/agent, finishedAt, status.
    - add evidence with model.
    - capabilities.
    - transcript.
  - catch:
    - failure classification: message; uncertain regex. `executeLatest(run:${id}:failure)`; set status UNKNOWN/FAILED, result, finishedAt. Keep model.
  - log? No secrets.
- cancel(operationId):
  - if active and sessionId: `guide.interrupt(sessionId)`. Then do NOT immediately mark CANCELLED? Interrupt makes wait resolve; completion may run. To guarantee UI response, mark CANCELLED via executeLatest action `run:${id}:cancel` and set `finishedAt`; then completion sees CANCELLED and preserves. Return.
  - if active without sessionId (still connecting), mark CANCELLED and state active will complete later but completion sees CANCELLED. Good.
  - if not active: only allow if status QUEUED/RUNNING; mark UNKNOWN? If server restarted, no session; we should require reconcile. But user can cancel a stale run safely: mark CANCELLED with note "No in-process runner; native session not interrupted." Is that lossy? The native session may still run. Better mark `UNKNOWN` and message "No in-process runner. Reconcile the native session before cancelling." But existing UI expects cancel. Requirement "support interrupt". We'll implement cancel active only; for inactive, throw `RUN_NOT_ACTIVE` with repair "Reconcile". On server startup, mark stale RUNNING/QUEUED as UNKNOWN.
- `reconcile` existing to inspect. If operation status QUEUED/RUNNING and inspect says running -> keep RUNNING, result. Good.
- `cancel` route returns project.
- `runMaker` returns `accepted` and operation. Need API status 202.

`reconcile` currently uses `guide.inspect` but guide constructed with password; fine. Add selected model not needed.

`recordUncertain` for guide speak failures. For Maker now handled by coordinator, not `recordUncertain`.

Update `runMaker` to not call recordUncertain.

`approve` etc unchanged.

`instructions` add selected model? Not necessary.

